#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Paints the Atlas, Navigation's painted map view (Doc/ideas/Navigation.md, *The painted view*), and shows how
far it is.

Both continents are cut into squares of one terrain tile at 2048 pixels (a quarter of a yard a pixel) that
overlap by an eighth. Each square with land is repainted by the local image model from the game's terrain, in
rows from the north-west, on its own (with painted neighbours pasted into its edges the model painted a garish
strip along the opposite edge): its words put together from the zone briefs and from what the game has in it
(dev/imagegen/maps/zones.toml, atlas.toml), the same light and look for all. Each painting is checked against
the game's water; one that fails is painted again with another seed, and the best of three is kept. The
squares are joined later, cut along the line where neighbours differ least.

Usage:
  dev/tools/atlas paint [--adt DIR --tables DIR --minimap DIR] [--continent 0|1] [--near ...] [--limit N]
                        [--out DIR] [--keep-comfyui] [--dry]
  dev/tools/atlas status [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/atlas join --to FILE [--near ...] [--continent 0|1] [--cut] [--out DIR]

  --adt, --tables, --minimap   the game's terrain files (<key>.adt), its client tables as CSV and its minimap
                               textures (<file id>.blp), as dev/tools/map-meta reads them; needed the first
                               time, then remembered in the run's state.json
  --continent                  only this continent (0 the Eastern Kingdoms, 1 Kalimdor; default both, in order)
  --limit N                    stop after N squares
  --out DIR                    the run's folder (default assets/generated/atlas)
  --keep-comfyui               leave ComfyUI running at the end (it is stopped again if this run started it)
  --dry                        paint nothing: the game's terrain stands in for each painting (to try the run)
  --near UIMAP X Y ACROSS DOWN only the squares around a point of a zone's map (its UiMap ID, x and y in
                               percent as on the game's map), ACROSS by DOWN of them (to try a city first)
  join                         the painted squares (of --near, or all of a continent) as one picture, FILE: each
                               overlap cut along the line where the two paintings differ least, softened a few
                               pixels to either side (--cut: cut in the overlap's middle instead, to compare)

While it runs in a terminal:
  p   pause after the picture being painted, and free the graphics card; p again goes on
  q   quit now: the picture being painted is dropped, every square finished is kept; the next run goes on
Without a terminal (its output to a file) it writes a line a square instead.

It needs ComfyUI (started if it is not running), and the guides of dev/tools/map-meta in
assets/generated/maps/meta. It writes, in the run's folder: <continent>/sq-<column>-<row>.png, the painting
kept for each square; state.json, the paths and per square its attempts (seed, water added and lost), the
one kept, its zone and its seconds; words.jsonl, the words each square was painted with.
"""
